// Curated, self-contained lesson content for the browser-only learning hub.
// Each example is annotated inline so learners can follow the code line by line.
window.PYTHON_LESSONS = [
  // LEVEL 1: PYTHON BASICS
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"1. Introduction to Python",explain:"Python is a readable, general-purpose language used for automation, websites, data, and AI. Python runs statements in order unless control-flow code changes that order.",code:`print("Python is ready")  # print displays text in the terminal`,output:`Python is ready`,mistake:"Python is case-sensitive: Print and print are different names.",challenge:"Print the words Hello, learner!.",answer:`print("Hello, learner!")`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"2. Installing Python and VS Code",explain:"Install Python 3 from python.org and VS Code from code.visualstudio.com. In VS Code, install the Python extension, select a Python interpreter, and run a saved .py file in the terminal.",code:`# Save this as hello.py, then run: python hello.py\nprint("Interpreter works")  # confirms Python can execute a file`,output:`Interpreter works`,mistake:"A VS Code editor alone is not the Python interpreter; check that python --version works in the terminal.",challenge:"What command prints your installed Python version?",answer:`python --version`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"3. First Python Program",explain:"A Python program is a sequence of instructions. A function call such as print(...) asks Python to perform an action.",code:`name = "Sukundra"  # store text in a variable\nprint("Hello,", name)  # display a greeting and the stored name`,output:`Hello, Sukundra`,mistake:"Text values need matching quotation marks; unmatched quotes cause a SyntaxError.",challenge:"Store your name in a variable and print a greeting.",answer:`name = "Asha"\nprint("Hello,", name)`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"4. Python Variables and Naming Rules",explain:"A variable is a name used to store a value in a program. You can use names such as name, student_age, age2, and _my_variable. Names cannot start with a number, contain spaces, or use Python keywords such as class. Names are case-sensitive, so age and Age are different.",code:`name = "Sukundra"\nage = 20\nheight = 5.7\nis_student = True\n\nprint(name)\nprint(age)\nprint(height)\nprint(is_student)\n\n# Valid names: name, student_age, age2, _my_variable\n# Invalid names: 2age, student name, class`,output:`Sukundra\n20\n5.7\nTrue`,mistake:"Do not start a variable name with a number, put spaces in a name, or use a keyword such as class.",challenge:"Create student_age with the value 20 and print it.",answer:`student_age = 20\nprint(student_age)`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"5. Python Strings and Data Types",explain:"A string (str) is a sequence of characters inside quotation marks. Python also has int for whole numbers, float for decimals, bool for True or False, list for an ordered changeable collection, tuple for an ordered unchangeable collection, set for unique values, dict for key-value pairs, NoneType for no value, and complex for real and imaginary parts.",code:`name = "Sukundra Pujara"\nmessage = 'Hello World'\nparagraph = """I am learning Python."""\nnumber_as_text = "123"\n\nprint(name)\nprint(type(name))`,output:`Sukundra Pujara\n<class 'str'>`,mistake:"Text must have matching quotes. A set does not guarantee item order; use a tuple for an ordered, fixed group.",challenge:"Create a string called message with the text Hello World and print its type.",answer:`message = "Hello World"\nprint(type(message))`,typeExamplesCode:`name = "Sukundra"  # str: text\nage = 20  # int: whole number\nprice = 99.50  # float: decimal\nis_student = True  # bool: True or False\nfruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Mango"]  # list: ordered, changeable\ncolors = ("Red", "Green", "Blue")  # tuple: ordered, unchangeable\nnumbers = {1, 2, 3, 4}  # set: unique values\nstudent = {"name": "Sukundra", "age": 20}  # dict: key-value pairs\nresult = None  # NoneType: no value\nnumber = 3 + 4j  # complex: real and imaginary parts\n\n# Additional built-in types:\nnumber_range = range(1, 6)\nbinary_data = b"Hello"  # bytes: immutable binary data\nchangeable_data = bytearray(5)  # mutable binary data\ndata_view = memoryview(b"Hi")  # view of binary data\nfixed_numbers = frozenset({1, 2, 3})  # immutable set`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"6. Check and Convert Data Types",explain:"Use type(value) to check a value's type. Type conversion changes a value to another type. Common conversions include int(), float(), str(), and bool().",code:`age = "20"\nage = int(age)  # convert text to an integer\nprice = float("99.5")  # convert text to a float\nnumber = str(100)  # convert integer to text\nvalue = bool(1)  # convert 1 to True\nprint(age)\nprint(price)\nprint(number)\nprint(value)`,output:`20\n99.5\n100\nTrue`,mistake:"int() cannot convert words such as 'twenty'. A non-empty string, including 'False', converts to True with bool().",challenge:"Convert the string '25' into an integer using int().",answer:`number = int("25")\nprint(number)`,theory:`Additional built-in types include range, bytes, bytearray, memoryview, and frozenset. range creates a number sequence; bytes is immutable binary data; bytearray is changeable binary data; memoryview views binary data; frozenset is an immutable set.`,typeCheckCode:`name = "Sukundra"\nage = 20\nheight = 5.7\nis_student = True\nfruits = ["Apple", "Mango"]\ncolors = ("Red", "Blue")\nnumbers = {1, 2, 3}\nstudent = {"name": "Sukundra"}\nresult = None\nnumber = 3 + 4j\n\nprint(type(name))\nprint(type(age))\nprint(type(height))\nprint(type(is_student))\nprint(type(fruits))\nprint(type(colors))\nprint(type(numbers))\nprint(type(student))\nprint(type(result))\nprint(type(number))`,completeCode:`name = "Sukundra Pujara"\nage = 20\nheight = 5.7\nis_student = True\nskills = ["Python", "HTML", "CSS"]\nsubjects = ("Python", "Mathematics")\nmarks = {80, 85, 90}\nstudent = {"name": "Sukundra", "age": 20}\nresult = None\ncomplex_number = 2 + 3j\n\nprint("Name:", name, type(name))\nprint("Age:", age, type(age))\nprint("Height:", height, type(height))\nprint("Student:", is_student, type(is_student))\nprint("Skills:", skills, type(skills))\nprint("Subjects:", subjects, type(subjects))\nprint("Marks:", marks, type(marks))\nprint("Student details:", student, type(student))\nprint("Result:", result, type(result))\nprint("Complex number:", complex_number, type(complex_number))`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"7. Input and Output",explain:"input() displays a prompt and returns the user's response as a string. Convert the result when your program needs a number.",code:`name = input("Name: ")  # wait for text from the user\nprint("Welcome,", name)  # show the response`,output:`If the user types Mina:\nName: Mina\nWelcome, Mina`,mistake:"input() returns str, so input('Age: ') + 1 is an error until the value is converted.",challenge:"Ask for a number and print it increased by one.",answer:`number = int(input("Number: "))\nprint(number + 1)`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"8. Operators",explain:"Arithmetic operators calculate values; comparison operators return booleans; and/or/not combine or reverse conditions. // is floor division, while % returns the remainder.",code:`total = 7 + 3  # addition\nis_even = total % 2 == 0  # remainder plus equality test\nprint(total, is_even, 7 // 2)`,output:`10 True 3`,mistake:"= assigns a value; == compares two values.",challenge:"Check whether 15 is divisible by 5.",answer:`print(15 % 5 == 0)  # True`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"9. Strings and String Methods",explain:"A string is a sequence of characters enclosed in quotation marks. You can use single quotes, double quotes, or triple quotes for longer text. Strings are text values, and type() reports their type as str.",code:`name = "Sukundra Pujara"\nmessage = 'Hello World'\nparagraph = """I am learning Python."""\nnumber_as_text = "123"\n\nprint(name)\nprint(type(name))`,output:`Sukundra Pujara\n<class 'str'>`,mistake:"Use matching quotation marks around text. The text '123' is a string because it is inside quotes.",challenge:"Store the text Python in a variable and print its type.",answer:`language = "Python"\nprint(type(language))`},
  {level:"Level 1 · Python Basics",category:"Beginner",title:"10. Comments and Indentation",explain:"A # starts a single-line comment. Indentation defines the body of compound statements, so statements in the same block use consistent spacing (commonly four spaces).",code:`score = 75  # a comment explains the value\nif score >= 50:  # colon begins a block\n    print("Pass")  # indentation puts this inside if`,output:`Pass`,mistake:"Inconsistent indentation or a missing colon causes an error; do not mix tabs and spaces.",challenge:"Write an if statement that prints Ready when a flag is True.",answer:`ready = True\nif ready:\n    print("Ready")`},
  // LEVEL 2: CONTROL FLOW
  {level:"Level 2 · Control Flow",category:"Beginner",title:"11. Conditional Statements: if, elif, else",explain:"Conditional statements let a program make decisions. if runs its block when its condition is True. elif (else if) is checked only when earlier conditions were False; you can use more than one elif. else runs when none of the conditions were True. It has no condition and is optional. Conditions are checked from top to bottom, and Python stops at the first true condition, so only one block in the chain runs. End each condition line with a colon and indent its block. Use comparison operators (==, !=, >, <, >=, <=) and logical operators (and, or, not) to create conditions.",code:`marks = 72  # the student's mark\n\nif marks >= 90:  # check the highest grade first\n    print("Grade A+")\nelif marks >= 80:  # checked only if the earlier condition was false\n    print("Grade A")\nelif marks >= 70:\n    print("Grade B")\nelif marks >= 60:\n    print("Grade C")\nelse:  # runs if every condition above was false\n    print("Fail")`,output:`Grade B`,mistake:"Conditions are checked from top to bottom. Put higher grade boundaries first, or a broad condition may match before a more specific one.",challenge:"Sukundra, set marks to 85 and use if/elif/else to print the correct grade using the lesson boundaries.",answer:`marks = 85\n\nif marks >= 90:\n    print("Grade A+")\nelif marks >= 80:\n    print("Grade A")\nelif marks >= 70:\n    print("Grade B")\nelif marks >= 60:\n    print("Grade C")\nelse:\n    print("Fail")`,commonRules:`- if runs its block only when its condition is True.\n- elif is checked only when earlier conditions were False. You can use many elif branches.\n- else runs when none of the conditions were True. It has no condition and is optional.\n- Conditions are checked top to bottom; Python stops at the first True condition, so only one block in the chain runs.\n- Put a colon at the end of each condition and indent the code inside its block.\n- Compare values with ==, !=, >, <, >=, and <=. Join checks with and, or, and not.`},
  {level:"Level 2 · Control Flow",category:"Beginner",title:"12. Nested Conditions",explain:"A nested condition is an if inside another if. The inner decision runs only when execution enters the outer block.",code:`logged_in = True  # outer condition\nis_admin = False  # inner condition's value\nif logged_in:  # first decision\n    if is_admin:  # decision inside the first branch\n        print("Admin tools")\n    else:\n        print("Member page")`,output:`Member page`,mistake:"Deep nesting is hard to follow; combine simple checks with and when that keeps the logic clearer.",challenge:"Print 'allowed' only when age is 18+ and has_id is True.",answer:`if age >= 18 and has_id:\n    print("allowed")`},
  {level:"Level 2 · Control Flow",category:"Beginner",title:"13. for Loops and range()",explain:"A for loop repeats its indented block once for each item in a sequence, such as a list, string, tuple, range, or dictionary. The loop variable takes the next item each time. The loop ends when there are no more items. Use a for loop when you want to visit items or repeat a known number of times. Put a colon at the end of the for line and indent its block. range(start, stop, step) makes numbers: start is included, stop is excluded, and step defaults to 1. A nested loop is a loop inside another loop: the inner loop completes all its turns for each turn of the outer loop. break exits early. continue skips to the next pass. An optional loop else runs only when the loop ends without break. A while loop repeats as long as its condition is true, so update its condition to make it stop.",code:`fruits = ["apple", "banana", "mango"]  # a list of items\n\nfor fruit in fruits:  # fruit gets the next item each time\n    print(fruit)  # indented code repeats for each fruit\n\nfor i in range(1, 10, 2):  # start 1, stop before 10, step by 2\n    print(i)\n\nfor i in range(5):  # starts at 0 and stops before 5\n    print(i)\n\n# The inner loop runs twice for each outer loop value\nfor i in range(3):\n    for j in range(2):\n        print(i, j)`,output:`apple\nbanana\nmango\n1\n3\n5\n7\n9\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\n0 0\n0 1\n1 0\n1 1\n2 0\n2 1`,mistake:"range() stops before its stop value. Remember the colon and indent the repeated code. The inner loop runs completely for each outer loop turn.",challenge:"Sukundra, use nested loops to print the pairs (0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0), and (1, 1).",answer:`for i in range(2):\n    for j in range(2):\n        print(i, j)`,loopRules:`- The loop variable receives the next item on each pass.\n- The loop ends automatically when the sequence runs out.\n- Add a colon after the for line and indent its repeated block.\n- range(start, stop, step) includes start and excludes stop. The default step is 1.\n- A nested loop is a loop inside another loop. The inner loop completes for each outer loop turn.\n- break exits the loop. continue skips to the next iteration.\n- An optional loop else runs only when the loop finishes without break.`,loopControlCode:`# continue skips 2; else runs because no break happened\nfor number in range(1, 4):\n    if number == 2:\n        continue\n    print(number)\nelse:\n    print("Finished without break")\n\n# break stops the loop, so its else does not run\nfor number in range(5):\n    if number == 2:\n        break\n    print(number)`,loopControlOutput:`1\n3\nFinished without break\n0\n1`},
  {level:"Level 2 · Control Flow",category:"Beginner",title:"14. break, continue, pass",explain:"break exits the nearest loop. continue skips the rest of the current iteration. pass is a placeholder statement that does nothing.",code:`for number in range(5):  # try values 0 to 4\n    if number == 1:\n        continue  # skip 1\n    if number == 4:\n        break  # stop before 4\n    print(number)`,output:`0\n2\n3`,mistake:"break exits the loop entirely; continue only skips one iteration.",challenge:"Print 1 through 5 but skip 3.",answer:`for number in range(1, 6):\n    if number == 3:\n        continue\n    print(number)`},
  {level:"Level 2 · Control Flow",category:"Beginner",title:"15. range() Function",explain:"range(stop) counts from zero up to, but not including, stop. range(start, stop, step) customizes the start and increment; it is commonly used in for loops.",code:`for number in range(2, 8, 2):  # start 2, stop before 8, step 2\n    print(number)`,output:`2\n4\n6`,mistake:"The stop value is excluded, so range(1, 5) ends at 4.",challenge:"Create a range that produces 3, 4, 5.",answer:`for number in range(3, 6):\n    print(number)`},
  {level:"Level 2 · Control Flow",category:"Beginner",title:"16. Pattern Printing",explain:"Nested loops can build rows and columns. The outer loop controls rows and the inner loop prints the repeated content in each row.",code:`for row in range(1, 4):  # choose row lengths 1 to 3\n    print("*" * row)  # repeat the star for that row`,output:`*\n**\n***`,mistake:"An off-by-one error in range() changes how many rows are printed.",challenge:"Print four rows containing one to four hashes.",answer:`for row in range(1, 5):\n    print("#" * row)`},
  {level:"Level 2 · Control Flow",category:"Beginner",title:"17. Match-Case Statements and Patterns",explain:"match compares a value with case patterns and runs the first matching case. It is a clear way to choose between several possibilities. Type patterns such as int() and float() check a value's type. A tuple pattern can save its items in names, and an if guard adds another condition. A dictionary pattern checks for the keys and values you write; other keys may still be present. The case _ pattern matches anything not matched earlier. match/case is available in Python 3.10 and later.",code:`command = "help"
match command:
    case "help":
        print("Show help")
    case "quit":
        print("Exit")
    case _:
        print("Unknown")

value = 3.14
match value:
    case int():
        print("Integer")
    case float():
        print("Float")
    case str():
        print("String")
    case list():
        print("List")
    case _:
        print("Other type")

point = (3, 3)
match point:
    case (0, 0):
        print("Origin")
    case (x, y) if x == y:
        print("On the diagonal")
    case (x, y) if x > y:
        print("Below the diagonal")
    case _:
        print("Above the diagonal")

user = {"role": "admin", "name": "Ram"}
match user:
    case {"role": "admin"}:
        print("Welcome admin")
    case {"role": "user"}:
        print("Welcome user")
    case _:
        print("Unknown role")`,output:`Show help
Float
On the diagonal
Welcome admin`,mistake:"match/case requires Python 3.10 or later. Put more specific patterns first, and remember that a dictionary pattern can match even when the dictionary has extra keys.",challenge:"Sukundra, use a type pattern to print 'String' when value is text, and use _ for every other type.",answer:`value = "Python"
match value:
    case str():
        print("String")
    case _:
        print("Other type")`,commonRules:`- match checks its value against case patterns from top to bottom.\n- The first matching case runs; later cases are skipped.\n- int(), float(), str(), and list() are examples of type patterns.\n- A tuple pattern can unpack values, such as (x, y).\n- Add if after a pattern to make a guard, such as case (x, y) if x == y:.\n- A dictionary pattern checks the listed key-value pairs and allows extra keys.\n- Use case _ as a fallback for values not matched earlier.\n- match/case requires Python 3.10 or newer.`},
  // LEVEL 3: DATA STRUCTURES
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"18. Lists and List Methods",explain:"A list is an ordered, changeable collection. append() adds one item, remove() deletes a matching value, and len() returns the number of items.",code:`scores = [70, 80]  # create a list\nscores.append(90)  # add at the end\nscores[0] = 75  # update the first item\nprint(scores, len(scores))`,output:`[75, 80, 90] 3`,mistake:"remove(value) removes by value; pop(index) removes and returns by position.",challenge:"Start with [2, 4], append 6, and print the list.",answer:`values = [2, 4]\nvalues.append(6)\nprint(values)`},
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"19. Tuples",explain:"A tuple is an ordered collection that cannot be changed after creation. Use an index such as student[0] to read an item; indexes start at 0. Use len() to count the tuple's items.",code:`student = ("Sukundra Pujara", 20, "Nepal", "Computer Science")\n\nprint("Student Details:")\nprint("Name:", student[0])\nprint("Age:", student[1])\nprint("Country:", student[2])\nprint("Course:", student[3])\n\nprint("Total Items:", len(student))`,output:`Student Details:\nName: Sukundra Pujara\nAge: 20\nCountry: Nepal\nCourse: Computer Science\nTotal Items: 4`,mistake:"Tuple indexes start at 0, so student[0] is the first item. Tuples cannot be changed after they are created.",challenge:"Create a tuple with your name and age. Print both values using their indexes and print the tuple's length.",answer:`student = ("Sukundra Pujara", 20)\nprint("Name:", student[0])\nprint("Age:", student[1])\nprint("Total Items:", len(student))`},
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"20. Sets",explain:"A set stores unique hashable values and is useful for membership checks and removing duplicates. Sets do not promise a display order.",code:`colors = {"red", "blue", "red"}  # duplicates collapse\ncolors.add("green")  # insert one value\nprint(len(colors), "blue" in colors)`,output:`3 True`,mistake:"Do not rely on set order; use sorted(my_set) when a predictable order is needed.",challenge:"Remove duplicates from [1, 1, 2] using a set.",answer:`unique_numbers = set([1, 1, 2])\nprint(len(unique_numbers))  # 2`},
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"21. Dictionaries",explain:"A dictionary maps unique keys to values. Use square brackets to access a known key or get(key, default) when a key may be absent.",code:`student = {"name": "Mina", "score": 88}  # key-value pairs\nstudent["score"] = 91  # update a value\nprint(student.get("name"), student.get("grade", "not set"))`,output:`Mina not set`,mistake:"Accessing a missing key with brackets raises KeyError; use get() when absence is expected.",challenge:"Create a dictionary with city 'Kathmandu' and print its value.",answer:`place = {"city": "Kathmandu"}\nprint(place["city"])`},
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"22. Indexing and Slicing",explain:"Sequence indexes begin at zero; negative indexes count from the end. A slice [start:stop:step] includes start and excludes stop.",code:`word = "PYTHON"  # six-character string\nprint(word[0], word[-1])  # first and last characters\nprint(word[1:4])  # characters at positions 1, 2, 3`,output:`P N\nYTH`,mistake:"The slice stop index is exclusive, and indexing past the end raises IndexError.",challenge:"Get the first three characters of 'computer'.",answer:`print("computer"[:3])  # com`},
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"23. List Comprehensions",explain:"A list comprehension creates a list from an iterable in one expression. Add an if clause to keep only matching values.",code:`squares = [n * n for n in range(1, 5)]  # transform each n\nodds = [n for n in range(6) if n % 2]  # keep odd n only\nprint(squares, odds)`,output:`[1, 4, 9, 16] [1, 3, 5]`,mistake:"Keep comprehensions readable; use a normal loop for complex branching or side effects.",challenge:"Build a list of doubled values for 1, 2, 3.",answer:`doubled = [n * 2 for n in [1, 2, 3]]\nprint(doubled)`},
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"24. Dictionary and Set Comprehensions",explain:"Comprehensions can create dictionaries with key:value and sets with braces. They apply an expression to each item and may filter with if.",code:`squares = {n: n * n for n in range(1, 4)}  # map n to square\ninitials = {name[0] for name in ["Mina", "Max"]}  # unique letters\nprint(squares, sorted(initials))`,output:`{1: 1, 2: 4, 3: 9} ['M']`,mistake:"A comprehension with key:value creates a dict; a set comprehension has a single expression.",challenge:"Create a dictionary mapping 1 and 2 to their cubes.",answer:`cubes = {n: n ** 3 for n in [1, 2]}\nprint(cubes)`},
  {level:"Level 3 · Data Structures",category:"Beginner",title:"25. Nested Data Structures",explain:"Lists and dictionaries can contain other collections. Access an inner value by chaining keys and indexes, and check that the shape matches your data.",code:`students = [{"name": "Ari", "marks": [80, 90]}]  # list of records\nfirst_mark = students[0]["marks"][0]  # list index, key, then index\nprint(first_mark)`,output:`80`,mistake:"A wrong index or key in nested data can raise IndexError or KeyError; inspect each layer.",challenge:"Read the city from {'address': {'city': 'Pokhara'}}.",answer:`person = {"address": {"city": "Pokhara"}}\nprint(person["address"]["city"])`},
  // LEVEL 4: FUNCTIONS
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"26. Defining and Calling Functions",explain:"def creates a reusable function. Its indented body runs only when the function is called.",code:`def greet():  # define a function\n    print("Welcome!")  # function body\n\ngreet()  # call it`,output:`Welcome!`,mistake:"Defining a function does not run it; call it by writing its name followed by parentheses.",challenge:"Define and call a function named say_hi that prints Hi.",answer:`def say_hi():\n    print("Hi")\nsay_hi()`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"27. Parameters and Arguments",explain:"A parameter is a named input in the function definition. An argument is the actual value supplied when calling the function.",code:`def greet(name):  # name is a parameter\n    print("Hello,", name)\n\ngreet("Laxmi")  # the string is an argument`,output:`Hello, Laxmi`,mistake:"A required parameter must receive an argument unless it has a default value.",challenge:"Write area(width, height) and print the area of 3 by 4.",answer:`def area(width, height):\n    return width * height\nprint(area(3, 4))`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"28. Return Statements",explain:"return sends a result back to the caller and ends that function call. print() displays a value but does not return it as a result.",code:`def double(number):  # accept one number\n    return number * 2  # send result to caller\n\nanswer = double(6)  # store returned result\nprint(answer)`,output:`12`,mistake:"A function without return returns None; do not confuse printing a result with returning it.",challenge:"Write a function that returns the square of 5.",answer:`def square(number):\n    return number ** 2\nprint(square(5))`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"29. Default and Keyword Arguments",explain:"A default parameter value is used when that argument is omitted. Keyword arguments name a parameter, making calls clearer and allowing flexible order.",code:`def greet(name, greeting="Hello"):  # default greeting\n    print(greeting, name)\n\ngreet("Mina")  # use default\ngreet(name="Ari", greeting="Namaste")  # keyword call`,output:`Hello Mina\nNamaste Ari`,mistake:"Parameters without defaults must come before parameters with defaults in a function definition.",challenge:"Create a function with a default currency 'NPR'.",answer:`def show_price(amount, currency="NPR"):\n    print(amount, currency)\nshow_price(50)`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"30. *args and **kwargs",explain:"*args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple. **kwargs collects extra named arguments into a dictionary.",code:`def show(*args, **kwargs):  # collect extra arguments\n    print(args)  # positional values become a tuple\n    print(kwargs)  # named values become a dictionary\n\nshow(2, 3, color="blue")`,output:`(2, 3)\n{'color': 'blue'}`,mistake:"Use the unpacking symbols in the function definition; argument names args and kwargs are conventions.",challenge:"Write a function that accepts any positional values and prints them.",answer:`def display(*values):\n    print(values)\ndisplay("a", "b")`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"31. Lambda Functions",explain:"A lambda creates a small anonymous function with one expression. Give it a name if you want to call it later. This example adds two numbers. Use def for logic that needs multiple statements or a descriptive reusable function.",code:`add = lambda a, b: a + b  # add two values\n\nprint(add(10, 20))  # call the lambda function`,output:`30`,mistake:"A lambda can contain only one expression. Use def when a function needs multiple steps or clearer documentation.",challenge:"Create a lambda named multiply that multiplies two numbers, then print the result for 4 and 5.",answer:`multiply = lambda a, b: a * b\nprint(multiply(4, 5))`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"32. map(), filter(), sorted()",explain:"filter() keeps items that pass a test. Here, lambda x: x % 2 == 0 is True for even numbers, so filter() keeps those numbers. Wrap the result in list() to see the values. map() can also convert every item, such as digit text, to an integer with map(int, numbers). sorted() creates a new sorted list; use reverse=True to sort from largest to smallest. The final example combines map(), filter(), and sorted() in one program.",code:`numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]\n\nresult = list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, numbers))\n\nprint(result)\n\n# Convert each string of digits into an integer\nnumbers = ["10", "20", "30", "40"]\nresult = list(map(int, numbers))\nprint(result)\n\nnumbers = [50, 10, 40, 20, 30]\nresult = sorted(numbers, reverse=True)\nprint(result)\n\n# 5. All Three in One Program\nnumbers = [5, 2, 8, 1, 6, 3, 4]\n\nmapped = list(map(lambda x: x * 2, numbers))\nfiltered = list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, numbers))\nsorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\nprint("Map:", mapped)\nprint("Filter:", filtered)\nprint("Sorted:", sorted_numbers)`,output:`[2, 4, 6, 8]\n[10, 20, 30, 40]\n[50, 40, 30, 20, 10]\nMap: [10, 4, 16, 2, 12, 6, 8]\nFilter: [2, 8, 6, 4]\nSorted: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8]`,mistake:"Use the same variable name when creating the list and passing it to filter(). map(int, numbers) converts numeric text to integers; the text must contain valid whole-number digits.",challenge:"Use filter to keep positive numbers from [-1, 2, 3].",answer:`positive = list(filter(lambda n: n > 0, [-1, 2, 3]))\nprint(positive)`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"33. Recursion",explain:"A recursive function calls itself on a smaller problem. A base case stops recursion; every recursive path should move toward that base case.",code:`def factorial(n):  # define recursive calculation\n    if n <= 1:  # base case stops calls\n        return 1\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)  # smaller problem\n\nprint(factorial(4))`,output:`24`,mistake:"Missing or unreachable base cases cause RecursionError; iteration may suit very deep tasks better.",challenge:"What does factorial(3) return?",answer:`6  # 3 * 2 * 1`},
  {level:"Level 4 · Functions",category:"Beginner",title:"34. Scope: Local and Global Variables",explain:"A local name exists inside its function. A global name is defined at module level; prefer passing values and returning results instead of changing globals.",code:`rate = 2  # module-level name\ndef calculate(value):  # function scope begins\n    result = value * rate  # read global rate\n    return result\nprint(calculate(5))`,output:`10`,mistake:"Assigning to a global name inside a function makes it local unless declared global; avoid unnecessary global state.",challenge:"Pass a rate as a function parameter instead of reading a global.",answer:`def calculate(value, rate):\n    return value * rate\nprint(calculate(5, 2))`},
  // LEVEL 5: INTERMEDIATE PYTHON
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"35. Modules and Packages",explain:"A module is a Python file. A package groups related modules in a directory, commonly with an __init__.py file. Split code when it becomes easier to reuse or navigate.",code:`# math is part of Python's standard library\nimport math  # load a module\nprint(math.ceil(2.2))  # use a function through its module`,output:`3`,mistake:"Do not name your own file after a standard module (for example, math.py), because it can shadow that module.",challenge:"Import random and print one integer from 1 to 3.",answer:`import random\nprint(random.randint(1, 3))`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"36. import Statements",explain:"import module makes names available through the module; from module import name imports a selected name. Aliases can make long module names easier to use.",code:`import math as m  # import module with alias\nfrom statistics import mean  # import one function\nprint(m.sqrt(9), mean([2, 4]))`,output:`3.0 3`,mistake:"Avoid wildcard imports (from module import *) because they hide where names come from.",challenge:"Import the datetime module as dt and print its date class name.",answer:`import datetime as dt\nprint(dt.date.__name__)`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"37. Exception Handling: try, except, else, finally",explain:"Put code that may fail in try. Handle expected errors in except, use else when no exception occurred, and use finally for cleanup that must always happen.",code:`try:  # code that may fail\n    value = int("12")\nexcept ValueError:  # handle a conversion error\n    print("Invalid number")\nelse:  # runs only when try succeeds\n    print(value)\nfinally:  # runs either way\n    print("Finished")`,output:`12\nFinished`,mistake:"Do not catch every error with a bare except; handle expected exception types and keep debugging information.",challenge:"Catch ValueError when converting 'abc' to int.",answer:`try:\n    int("abc")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Please enter digits")`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"38. File Handling: Read, Write, Append",explain:"open() accesses a file. Use a with block so Python closes it automatically. Mode w replaces content, a appends, and r reads.",code:`from pathlib import Path  # safe path utilities\npath = Path("sample.txt")\nwith path.open("w", encoding="utf-8") as file:  # write text\n    file.write("First line")\nwith path.open(encoding="utf-8") as file:  # default mode is read\n    print(file.read())`,output:`First line`,mistake:"Opening a file with mode w overwrites its previous contents; use a when you mean to append.",challenge:"Append the text 'Next' to a file using a context manager.",answer:`with open("sample.txt", "a", encoding="utf-8") as file:\n    file.write("Next\\n")`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"39. Working with CSV and JSON",explain:"CSV stores rows and columns as text; use csv.DictReader for named columns. JSON represents common data as text and converts to Python values with json.loads/dumps.",code:`import json  # use Python's JSON tools\nrecord = {"name": "Ari", "active": True}\ntext = json.dumps(record)  # Python dict to JSON text\nrestored = json.loads(text)  # JSON text back to dict\nprint(restored["name"])`,output:`Ari`,mistake:"JSON does not support every Python type (such as sets); use JSON-compatible values and handle malformed input.",challenge:"Encode a dict with a count of 3 as JSON.",answer:`import json\nprint(json.dumps({"count": 3}))`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"40. Object-Oriented Programming",explain:"Object-oriented programming groups state and behavior. Objects model concepts in your program; use a class when instances need their own data and related methods.",code:`class Lamp:  # define a type of object\n    def turn_on(self):  # behavior available to each lamp\n        return "Light on"\nlamp = Lamp()  # create an instance\nprint(lamp.turn_on())`,output:`Light on`,mistake:"Do not use a class just to wrap one simple function; a small function may be clearer.",challenge:"Make an object called dog from an empty Dog class.",answer:`class Dog:\n    pass\ndog = Dog()`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"41. Classes and Objects",explain:"A class describes a type; an object (instance) is one value of that type. Attributes store state and methods implement behavior.",code:`class Student:  # class definition\n    pass\n\nstudent = Student()  # instantiate the class\nstudent.name = "Rita"  # attach an instance attribute\nprint(student.name)`,output:`Rita`,mistake:"Each instance should normally own its changing state; do not accidentally share mutable class attributes.",challenge:"Create two objects from a Book class.",answer:`class Book:\n    pass\nfirst = Book()\nsecond = Book()`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"42. Constructors and self",explain:"__init__ initializes a new instance. self refers to that particular instance and is written first in instance method definitions; Python passes it automatically.",code:`class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name):  # initialize each new object\n        self.name = name  # save state on this instance\n\nstudent = Student("Rita")  # name is supplied to __init__\nprint(student.name)`,output:`Rita`,mistake:"Write self in the method definition but do not pass it manually when calling student.method().",challenge:"Create a Car class that stores a model.",answer:`class Car:\n    def __init__(self, model):\n        self.model = model\ncar = Car("EV")\nprint(car.model)`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"43. Inheritance",explain:"Inheritance lets a child class reuse and specialize a parent class. Call super().__init__() when the child needs the parent's initialization.",code:`class Animal:\n    def speak(self):  # shared parent behavior\n        return "sound"\nclass Dog(Animal):  # Dog inherits Animal\n    pass\ndog = Dog()\nprint(dog.speak())`,output:`sound`,mistake:"Use inheritance for a genuine 'is a' relationship; composition is often simpler for 'has a' relationships.",challenge:"Create a Cat class inheriting from Animal.",answer:`class Cat(Animal):\n    pass\ncat = Cat()\nprint(cat.speak())`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"44. Encapsulation and Polymorphism",explain:"Encapsulation keeps an object's data and operations together; a leading underscore signals an internal attribute by convention. Polymorphism lets different objects implement the same method interface.",code:`class Circle:\n    def area(self):  # one shape implementation\n        return 12\nclass Square:\n    def area(self):  # same interface, different shape\n        return 9\nfor shape in [Circle(), Square()]:\n    print(shape.area())`,output:`12\n9`,mistake:"A leading underscore does not enforce privacy in Python; treat internal names as an API convention.",challenge:"Add a Triangle class with an area() method returning 6.",answer:`class Triangle:\n    def area(self):\n        return 6`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"45. Class Methods and Static Methods",explain:"A class method receives the class as cls and often creates alternate constructors. A static method receives neither self nor cls and groups a related utility.",code:`class User:\n    @staticmethod  # utility has no instance state\n    def valid_name(name):\n        return bool(name.strip())\nprint(User.valid_name("Ari"))`,output:`True`,mistake:"Use @classmethod when behavior needs the class; use @staticmethod only when neither instance nor class state is required.",challenge:"Add a static method that doubles a number.",answer:`class MathTools:\n    @staticmethod\n    def double(number):\n        return number * 2\nprint(MathTools.double(4))`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"46. Iterators and Generators",explain:"An iterator supplies values one at a time. A generator function uses yield to produce values lazily, which can save memory for large sequences.",code:`def countdown(start):  # generator function\n    while start > 0:\n        yield start  # pause and produce one value\n        start -= 1\nprint(list(countdown(3)))  # consume the generator`,output:`[3, 2, 1]`,mistake:"A generator is usually consumed once; create a new generator if you need to iterate its values again.",challenge:"Write a generator that yields 1, then 2.",answer:`def two_values():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\nprint(list(two_values()))`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"47. Decorators",explain:"A decorator wraps a function to add behavior without changing its call site. The @ syntax applies a decorator when the function is defined.",code:`def announce(function):  # accept a function\n    def wrapper():  # replacement call behavior\n        print("Starting")\n        return function()  # run original function\n    return wrapper\n@announce\ndef greet():\n    print("Hello")\ngreet()`,output:`Starting\nHello`,mistake:"Use functools.wraps in production decorators to preserve the wrapped function's name and documentation.",challenge:"Write a decorator that prints 'Before' then calls a no-argument function.",answer:`def before(function):\n    def wrapper():\n        print("Before")\n        return function()\n    return wrapper`},
  {level:"Level 5 · Intermediate Python",category:"Intermediate",title:"48. Virtual Environments and pip",explain:"A virtual environment isolates project packages. pip installs packages into the currently active environment; record dependencies so another developer can reproduce the setup.",code:`# Windows PowerShell commands (run in your project folder)\npy -m venv .venv  # create isolated environment\n.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1  # activate in PowerShell\npy -m pip install requests  # install a package`,output:`A virtual environment is created; pip reports the installed package version.`,mistake:"If packages install to the wrong Python, activate the project environment and use python -m pip.",challenge:"What command creates a requirements file from installed packages?",answer:`python -m pip freeze > requirements.txt`},
  // LEVEL 6: ADVANCED PYTHON
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"49. Advanced Comprehensions",explain:"Nested comprehensions can transform nested iterables and apply filters. Use them only when the expression remains easy to understand; otherwise use explicit loops.",code:`matrix = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]  # rows of values\nflat = [value for row in matrix for value in row]  # flatten rows\nprint(flat)`,output:`[1, 2, 3, 4]`,mistake:"Deeply nested comprehensions are difficult to debug; break complicated transformations into named steps.",challenge:"Flatten [[5], [6, 7]] into one list.",answer:`flat = [value for row in [[5], [6, 7]] for value in row]\nprint(flat)`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"50. Closures",explain:"A closure is an inner function that remembers values from its enclosing scope after the outer function returns. nonlocal allows the inner function to rebind an enclosing local name.",code:`def make_counter():\n    count = 0  # enclosing variable\n    def next_value():\n        nonlocal count  # rebind enclosing count\n        count += 1\n        return count\n    return next_value  # return inner function\ncounter = make_counter()\nprint(counter(), counter())`,output:`1 2`,mistake:"Without nonlocal, assigning count inside next_value creates a new local and reading it first can raise UnboundLocalError.",challenge:"Create a closure that remembers a starting number and adds one each call.",answer:`def counter_from(start):\n    def next_value():\n        nonlocal start\n        start += 1\n        return start\n    return next_value`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"51. Higher-Order Functions",explain:"A higher-order function accepts a function, returns one, or both. This pattern supports reusable transformations such as sorting with a custom key.",code:`people = [{"name": "Bo"}, {"name": "Ari"}]  # records\nby_name = sorted(people, key=lambda person: person["name"])  # callback chooses sort key\nprint([person["name"] for person in by_name])`,output:`['Ari', 'Bo']`,mistake:"A sort key should return comparable values consistently; do not write key=callback() unless you mean to call it immediately.",challenge:"Sort ['pear', 'fig'] by word length.",answer:`print(sorted(["pear", "fig"], key=len))`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"52. Context Managers",explain:"A context manager manages setup and cleanup around a with block. Files are a common example; custom managers implement __enter__ and __exit__ or use contextlib.",code:`from contextlib import contextmanager  # helper for managers\n@contextmanager\ndef label():\n    print("enter")  # setup\n    yield  # run the with-block here\n    print("exit")  # cleanup\nwith label():\n    print("work")`,output:`enter\nwork\nexit`,mistake:"Put cleanup in a finally-safe context manager so exceptions inside the with block still release resources.",challenge:"Name the keyword used to enter a context manager.",answer:`with`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"53. Regular Expressions",explain:"Regular expressions describe text patterns. re.search finds a match; raw strings (r'...') make backslashes easier to read.",code:`import re  # regular-expression module\nmatch = re.search(r"\\d+", "Room 204")  # find digit run\nprint(match.group())  # extract matched text`,output:`204`,mistake:"A regex match may be None; check it before calling group(). Use a raw string for most patterns.",challenge:"Find the first sequence of digits in 'A12B'.",answer:`match = re.search(r"\\d+", "A12B")\nprint(match.group())  # 12`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"54. Type Hints",explain:"Type hints document expected types and help static analysis tools catch mismatches. Python does not normally enforce annotations at runtime.",code:`def greet(name: str) -> str:  # input and return hints\n    return f"Hello, {name}"\nmessage: str = greet("Ari")  # annotated variable\nprint(message)`,output:`Hello, Ari`,mistake:"An annotation is not automatic validation; validate external data at runtime when correctness depends on it.",challenge:"Annotate a function that accepts two integers and returns an integer.",answer:`def add(left: int, right: int) -> int:\n    return left + right`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"55. Dataclasses",explain:"@dataclass generates common methods such as __init__ and __repr__ for classes primarily used to hold data.",code:`from dataclasses import dataclass  # import decorator\n@dataclass\nclass Point:\n    x: int  # field becomes an init parameter\n    y: int\npoint = Point(2, 5)  # generated constructor\nprint(point)`,output:`Point(x=2, y=5)`,mistake:"Use default_factory for mutable defaults such as lists so each instance receives its own list.",challenge:"Make a dataclass Item with a string name.",answer:`from dataclasses import dataclass\n@dataclass\nclass Item:\n    name: str`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"56. Collections Module",explain:"collections contains specialized containers. Counter counts values, defaultdict supplies missing-value defaults, and deque supports efficient appends at both ends.",code:`from collections import Counter  # import frequency counter\ncounts = Counter("banana")  # count each character\nprint(counts["a"], counts.most_common(1))`,output:`3 [('a', 3)]`,mistake:"A defaultdict can create a value on missing-key access; use it only when that behavior is intended.",challenge:"Use Counter to count the letters in 'aba'.",answer:`from collections import Counter\nprint(Counter("aba")["a"])  # 2`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"57. itertools and functools",explain:"itertools provides memory-efficient iterator building blocks. functools offers tools such as reduce, partial, and cache; prefer the clearest tool for the task.",code:`from itertools import chain  # join iterables lazily\nfrom functools import reduce  # combine values\nvalues = chain([1, 2], [3])\ntotal = reduce(lambda left, right: left + right, values)\nprint(total)`,output:`6`,mistake:"reduce can obscure simple logic; sum() is clearer for adding numbers.",challenge:"Use itertools.chain to iterate over [1] and [2, 3].",answer:`from itertools import chain\nprint(list(chain([1], [2, 3])))`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"58. Multithreading and Multiprocessing",explain:"Threads share one process and suit many I/O waits. Processes have separate memory and can use multiple CPU cores for CPU-heavy work, with extra startup and communication costs.",code:`from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor  # thread pool\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor() as pool:  # clean up workers\n    results = list(pool.map(str.upper, ["a", "b"]))  # run I/O-style tasks concurrently\nprint(results)`,output:`['A', 'B']`,mistake:"Concurrent code can have race conditions; protect shared mutable state and benchmark before adding concurrency.",challenge:"Which is generally a better fit for waiting on many network responses: threads or processes?",answer:`Threads are commonly suitable for I/O-bound waiting tasks.`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"59. Asyncio and Async/Await",explain:"async def defines a coroutine. await pauses that coroutine while an asynchronous operation completes, allowing an event loop to run other tasks.",code:`import asyncio  # asynchronous runtime\nasync def greet():  # define coroutine\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)  # yield control to event loop\n    return "Hello"\nprint(asyncio.run(greet()))  # start event loop`,output:`Hello`,mistake:"Calling an async function creates a coroutine; it does not run until awaited or scheduled on an event loop.",challenge:"What keyword pauses an async function while awaiting a result?",answer:`await`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"60. Memory Management and Garbage Collection",explain:"Python manages object references automatically and reclaims unreachable objects. The gc module can inspect or collect reference cycles, but normal code rarely needs manual collection.",code:`import gc  # garbage-collection tools\nvalues = [1, 2, 3]  # list object has a reference\nvalues = None  # drop this reference\nprint(gc.isenabled())  # check automatic cyclic GC`,output:`True`,mistake:"Do not rely on garbage collection timing to close files or sockets; use context managers.",challenge:"Which language construct reliably closes a file after use?",answer:`A with statement, such as with open(...) as file:`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"61. Python Performance and Profiling",explain:"Measure before optimizing. timeit compares small snippets; cProfile identifies where a complete program spends time.",code:`import timeit  # small benchmark helper\nseconds = timeit.timeit("sum(range(100))", number=1000)  # repeat test\nprint(seconds > 0)  # a measured duration is positive`,output:`True`,mistake:"One timing run is noisy; use representative workloads and profiling evidence rather than guessing.",challenge:"Name Python's built-in module for profiling a program.",answer:`cProfile`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"62. Unit Testing and Debugging",explain:"Unit tests check small behaviors repeatedly. unittest is built in; assertions compare expected and actual results, and debuggers let you inspect state step by step.",code:`import unittest  # standard testing library\nclass TestMath(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add(self):  # test method starts with test\n        self.assertEqual(2 + 3, 5)  # expected result\nunittest.main(argv=[""], exit=False)  # run this example`,output:`Ran 1 test ... OK`,mistake:"A test that only repeats implementation details may miss behavior; test outcomes and important edge cases.",challenge:"Which unittest assertion checks that two values are equal?",answer:`self.assertEqual(actual, expected)`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"63. Logging",explain:"The logging module records events with severity levels. Logging can be filtered and routed to files or consoles, unlike print statements meant for casual output.",code:`import logging  # standard logging tools\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format="%(levelname)s: %(message)s")  # set output format\nlogging.info("App started")  # record an informational event`,output:`INFO: App started`,mistake:"Do not log passwords, tokens, or other sensitive values; log useful context without secrets.",challenge:"Which logging level is intended for error conditions?",answer:`logging.ERROR`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"64. Working with APIs using requests",explain:"An HTTP API exchanges requests and responses. requests is a third-party library; set a timeout, check the status, and validate the response before using its data.",code:`# Install first: python -m pip install requests\nimport requests  # third-party HTTP client\n# response = requests.get("https://example.com/data", timeout=10)\n# response.raise_for_status()  # report non-success HTTP status\n# data = response.json()  # parse JSON response\nprint("Use a timeout and check the response")`,output:`Use a timeout and check the response`,mistake:"Never assume a network request succeeds; handle timeouts, HTTP errors, and unexpected response data.",challenge:"Which requests.get argument sets a maximum wait time?",answer:`timeout, for example requests.get(url, timeout=10)`},
  {level:"Level 6 · Advanced Python",category:"Advanced",title:"65. Environment Variables and Secure Configuration",explain:"Environment variables configure software outside source code. os.environ reads required settings; never commit passwords, tokens, or private keys to a repository.",code:`import os  # access environment settings\nmode = os.environ.get("APP_MODE", "development")  # default if absent\nprint(mode)`,output:`development  # when APP_MODE is not set`,mistake:"A default is not appropriate for secrets; fail clearly when a required secret is missing, and keep it out of logs.",challenge:"Read PORT from the environment, defaulting to '5000'.",answer:`port = os.environ.get("PORT", "5000")`},
  // LEVEL 7: DATABASES AND DEVELOPMENT
  {level:"Level 7 · Databases and Development",category:"Intermediate",title:"66. SQL and Python Database Connections",explain:"SQL retrieves and changes structured records. A Python database connection manages queries and transactions; always bind user-provided values as parameters.",code:`# SQL concept: SELECT name FROM learner WHERE id = ?\nquery = "SELECT name FROM learner WHERE id = ?"  # parameter placeholder\nuser_id = 7  # bind value separately; do not concatenate input\nprint(query, user_id)`,output:`SELECT name FROM learner WHERE id = ? 7`,mistake:"Never build SQL by joining raw user input into the query; parameterized statements help prevent SQL injection.",challenge:"Why use a placeholder in a database query?",answer:`It binds data separately from SQL syntax and avoids unsafe string concatenation.`},
  {level:"Level 7 · Databases and Development",category:"Intermediate",title:"67. SQLite Basics",explain:"SQLite stores a database in a local file and needs no separate database server. Python's sqlite3 module is included with Python; commit successful changes and close connections.",code:`import sqlite3  # built-in SQLite driver\nwith sqlite3.connect(":memory:") as db:  # temporary in-memory database\n    db.execute("CREATE TABLE scores (value INTEGER)")\n    db.execute("INSERT INTO scores VALUES (?)", (9,))  # bind a value\n    print(db.execute("SELECT value FROM scores").fetchone()[0])`,output:`9`,mistake:"Bind values using ? placeholders, and do not assume a transaction is saved if it was rolled back or never committed.",challenge:"Which Python standard-library module connects to SQLite?",answer:`sqlite3`},
  {level:"Level 7 · Databases and Development",category:"Intermediate",title:"68. MySQL Fundamentals",explain:"MySQL is a client-server relational database. Python typically connects through an installed MySQL driver; credentials belong in secure configuration, not code.",code:`# Install a driver such as mysql-connector-python separately.\n# Use a parameterized query with the driver's placeholder style:\nquery = "SELECT name FROM learners WHERE id = %s"  # placeholder example\nprint("Keep connection credentials outside source code")`,output:`Keep connection credentials outside source code`,mistake:"Placeholder syntax varies by driver; follow that driver's parameter binding documentation instead of string formatting SQL.",challenge:"Should a database password be committed in a public Python file?",answer:`No. Store credentials in protected environment variables or a secret manager.`},
  {level:"Level 7 · Databases and Development",category:"Intermediate",title:"69. Flask Introduction",explain:"Flask is a lightweight Python web framework. A route connects a URL to a Python view function; Flask is an external dependency, not part of this HTML/CSS/JavaScript website.",code:`# Flask example (install Flask in a Python environment first)\nfrom flask import Flask  # import framework\napp = Flask(__name__)  # create application\n@app.get("/")  # map GET / to this function\ndef home():\n    return "Hello from Flask"\nprint("Run the app with Flask's development command")`,output:`Run the app with Flask's development command`,mistake:"Do not deploy Flask's development server as a production server; use an appropriate production WSGI server.",challenge:"What Flask decorator maps a function to a URL route?",answer:`@app.route(...) or a method-specific decorator such as @app.get(...)`},
  {level:"Level 7 · Databases and Development",category:"Intermediate",title:"70. Django Introduction",explain:"Django is a full-featured Python web framework with URL routing, views, templates, an ORM, and many built-in web features. It is installed separately.",code:`# Typical Django workflow commands\n# python -m pip install django\n# django-admin startproject config .\n# python manage.py runserver\nprint("Django projects organize routes, views, templates, and models")`,output:`Django projects organize routes, views, templates, and models`,mistake:"Keep development and production settings separate; never expose Django's debug mode publicly.",challenge:"Name Django's built-in object-relational mapper abbreviation.",answer:`ORM`},
  {level:"Level 7 · Databases and Development",category:"Intermediate",title:"71. REST API Fundamentals",explain:"A REST-style API exposes resources through HTTP. GET commonly reads, POST creates, PUT/PATCH updates, and DELETE removes. Status codes describe the result.",code:`# Example resource request concept\nmethod = "GET"  # read a resource\npath = "/api/learners/7"  # identify one learner\nstatus = 200  # successful HTTP response\nprint(method, path, status)`,output:`GET /api/learners/7 200`,mistake:"Do not put secrets in URLs; URLs can be recorded in browser history and server logs.",challenge:"Which common HTTP method is used to request resource data?",answer:`GET`},
  {level:"Level 7 · Databases and Development",category:"Intermediate",title:"72. Git and GitHub Basics",explain:"Git tracks changes in a repository; GitHub hosts Git repositories online. A commit records a snapshot, and a remote lets you share changes.",code:`# Common Git commands in a project folder\ngit status  # inspect working tree\ngit add index.html  # stage a file\ngit commit -m "Add lesson notes"  # record staged changes`,output:`Git reports the status, then creates a commit when configured.`,mistake:"Review staged changes before committing; never commit passwords, tokens, or private files.",challenge:"Which command shows changed and staged files?",answer:`git status`},
  // LEVEL 8: PYTHON PROJECTS
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Beginner",title:"73. Calculator",explain:"A calculator project combines input, numeric conversion, branching, and arithmetic. Start with a small function and guard against division by zero.",code:`def calculate(a, operator, b):  # receive operation data\n    if operator == "+": return a + b  # add values\n    if operator == "-": return a - b  # subtract values\n    if operator == "*": return a * b  # multiply values\n    if operator == "/" and b != 0: return a / b  # safe division\n    return "Unsupported operation"\nprint(calculate(8, "/", 2))`,output:`4.0`,mistake:"Check for a zero divisor before division and validate that the requested operator is supported.",challenge:"What should calculate(8, '/', 0) do?",answer:`Return a clear error or message instead of attempting division by zero.`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Beginner",title:"74. Number Guessing Game",explain:"A guessing game uses random numbers, repeated input, comparisons, and a stopping condition. Keep the target secret and give helpful higher/lower hints.",code:`target = 6  # fixed target keeps this example predictable\nguess = 4  # pretend this was entered by a player\nif guess < target:  # compare guess and target\n    print("Try higher")`,output:`Try higher`,mistake:"Convert user input to an integer and handle invalid text instead of letting the game crash.",challenge:"Add a branch that prints 'Correct' when guess equals target.",answer:`if guess == target:\n    print("Correct")`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Intermediate",title:"75. Student Management System",explain:"Represent each student with a dictionary or class and store many students in a collection. Add functions for create, find, update, and list operations.",code:`students = {"S01": {"name": "Mina", "score": 86}}  # keyed records\nstudent = students.get("S01")  # find safely by ID\nprint(student["name"], student["score"])`,output:`Mina 86`,mistake:"Use a stable unique ID rather than a student's name as the only identifier; names may repeat.",challenge:"Add a record with id S02 and name Ari.",answer:`students["S02"] = {"name": "Ari", "score": 0}`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Intermediate",title:"76. ATM Simulation",explain:"An ATM simulation practices state and validation. Store a balance, validate withdrawal amounts, and update the balance only after all checks pass.",code:`balance = 100  # starting balance\namount = 30  # requested withdrawal\nif 0 < amount <= balance:  # validate before changing state\n    balance -= amount  # subtract accepted withdrawal\nprint(balance)`,output:`70`,mistake:"Never subtract an invalid amount or allow a negative withdrawal; validate all conditions first.",challenge:"What balance remains after withdrawing 40 from 100?",answer:`60`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Beginner",title:"77. To-Do List",explain:"A to-do app stores task text and completion state. Start with a list, then add commands to add, show, complete, and remove tasks.",code:`tasks = []  # begin with no tasks\ntasks.append({"text": "Study Python", "done": False})  # add task\ntasks[0]["done"] = True  # mark it complete\nprint(tasks[0])`,output:`{'text': 'Study Python', 'done': True}`,mistake:"Update a task by its ID or selected position; avoid silently changing the wrong item.",challenge:"Add a second incomplete task called 'Practice loops'.",answer:`tasks.append({"text": "Practice loops", "done": False})`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Intermediate",title:"78. File Organizer",explain:"A file organizer groups files by extension. pathlib makes paths readable; test on a temporary folder and avoid moving files without a backup or preview.",code:`from pathlib import Path  # path handling tools\nfile = Path("report.txt")  # example file path\nextension = file.suffix.lower()  # get normalized extension\nfolder = extension.lstrip(".") or "no_extension"  # choose folder name\nprint(folder)`,output:`txt`,mistake:"A bulk organizer can overwrite or move important files; preview changes and handle duplicate names first.",challenge:"What folder label does Path('photo.PNG').suffix.lower() produce after removing the dot?",answer:`png`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Intermediate",title:"79. Weather API Application",explain:"A weather app accepts a location, calls a weather API, parses JSON, and displays selected fields. Protect API keys and handle unavailable locations and network failures.",code:`# Typical response after a successful API request\nweather = {"city": "Pokhara", "temperature": 22}\nprint(f"{weather['city']}: {weather['temperature']} C")  # format result`,output:`Pokhara: 22 C`,mistake:"Do not hard-code or publish a private API key; use a secret configuration and set request timeouts.",challenge:"Format a dictionary's city and temperature into one readable line.",answer:`print(f"{weather['city']}: {weather['temperature']} C")`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Intermediate",title:"80. Flask Web Application",explain:"A Flask app maps routes to views, renders templates, validates forms, and may use a database. Keep request handling and data logic organized.",code:`# Minimal Flask route example (requires Flask)\n@app.get("/hello")  # respond to GET /hello\ndef hello():  # route handler\n    return "Hello, web!"  # response body\nprint("A browser can request the /hello route")`,output:`A browser can request the /hello route`,mistake:"Validate submitted data on the server too; browser-side validation alone can be bypassed.",challenge:"What does a Flask route function return to the client?",answer:`A response, often a string, template, or response object.`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Advanced",title:"81. Django Blog Application",explain:"A blog project models posts and authors, exposes views, renders templates, and uses Django's ORM. Add validation and access controls before publishing.",code:`# Conceptual Django model example\nclass Post(models.Model):  # database-backed blog record\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=120)  # short required text\n    body = models.TextField()  # longer article content\nprint("Create migrations after changing models")`,output:`Create migrations after changing models`,mistake:"Changing a model requires migrations; do not edit production database tables manually without a migration plan.",challenge:"Which command creates migration files from model changes?",answer:`python manage.py makemigrations`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Advanced",title:"82. REST API Project",explain:"A REST API project defines resource routes, validates JSON input, returns suitable status codes, and tests success and failure cases.",code:`# Example API response data\nresponse = {"id": 7, "name": "Ari"}  # represent a resource\nstatus_code = 200  # indicate successful read\nprint(response["name"], status_code)`,output:`Ari 200`,mistake:"Return an appropriate error status for invalid input; do not report every request as successful.",challenge:"Which common status code means a resource was created?",answer:`201 Created`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Advanced",title:"83. Data Analysis Project",explain:"A data analysis workflow loads data, checks missing or unexpected values, computes summaries, and communicates findings. Keep a reproducible record of cleaning steps.",code:`scores = [72, 81, 87]  # small sample dataset\naverage = sum(scores) / len(scores)  # compute mean\nprint(round(average, 1))  # readable summary`,output:`80.0`,mistake:"Averages can hide outliers and missing data; inspect the distribution and explain how data was cleaned.",challenge:"Find the largest score in [72, 81, 87].",answer:`print(max([72, 81, 87]))  # 87`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Advanced",title:"84. Cybersecurity Log Analyzer",explain:"A defensive log analyzer parses authorized logs, counts event types, and flags suspicious patterns for review. Minimize retained personal data and avoid treating a flag as proof.",code:`events = ["login_ok", "login_fail", "login_fail"]  # sample authorized log events\nfailures = events.count("login_fail")  # count failures\nif failures >= 2:  # simple review threshold\n    print("Review repeated login failures")`,output:`Review repeated login failures`,mistake:"Logs can contain sensitive data; restrict access, redact secrets, and investigate alerts with context.",challenge:"Count the number of 'error' entries in ['ok', 'error', 'error'].",answer:`events = ["ok", "error", "error"]\nprint(events.count("error"))  # 2`},
  {level:"Level 8 · Python Projects",category:"Intermediate",title:"85. Automation Script",explain:"An automation script repeats a safe, well-defined task. Use pathlib, clear logs, dry-run options, and cautious error handling before changing many files.",code:`from pathlib import Path  # file-system paths\nfolder = Path("reports")  # target directory\nfiles = list(folder.glob("*.txt"))  # preview matching files\nprint(len(files))  # inspect count before acting`,output:`The number of .txt files in reports (for example, 3)`,mistake:"Do not automate destructive file operations without a dry run, a backup, and careful path checks.",challenge:"Find all CSV files in a folder named data without modifying them.",answer:`from pathlib import Path\ncsv_files = list(Path("data").glob("*.csv"))\nprint(csv_files)`}
];
